= Related Work <related-work>

Mathematical decomposition techniques for mixed-integer linear programming are well established within the optimization community. Methods such as Benders decomposition @benders1962, Lagrangian relaxation @fisher1981 @geoffrion1974 @everett1963 @lemarechal2001 and Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition @dantzig1960 operate at the solver level by exploiting algebraic structure in the constraint matrix @lasdon1970 @bertsekas1999. These techniques are domain-agnostic, applying generically to any @MILP formulation without leveraging knowledge about what the variables and constraints represent. A related research question for supply chain optimization is how to decompose problems at the domain level, exploiting the network structure that defines how materials flow from suppliers through production stages to customers.

SAP Integrated Business Planning incorporates heuristic decomposition approaches to manage the computational complexity of supply network planning @stadtler2015scm @dickersbach2009sap. Sandner @sandner1998 provides the foundational reference for SAP's decomposition strategies. These methods build upon classical decomposition theory @dantzig1963 but adapt it for the practical constraints of mixed-integer formulations where exact methods cannot be directly applied due to integrality requirements @schewe2020decomposition @gunther2009planning. Four primary decomposition strategies are implemented: Priority Decomposition, Time Decomposition, Resource Decomposition and Product Decomposition. Priority Decomposition partitions demands by priority class, solving subproblems in descending order to ensure high-priority demands claim capacities first. Time Decomposition partitions along the temporal dimension, taking subsets of time buckets successively into the planning window while aggregating future periods into coarser buckets @jackson2003. This rolling horizon approach relates conceptually to the multi-period planning structures studied in operations research generally @neumann1993. Resource Decomposition partitions the problem based on subsets of resources, grouping production models and materials that depend on the selected resources @sandner1998. Product Decomposition partitions along the material dimension, drawing on concepts from decentralized planning @williams1991. A preprocessing phase analyzes the material dependency graph to find connected components and resources are allocated across subproblems using a first-come, first-served strategy. The key structural characteristic is that Product Decomposition cuts horizontally through the supply network, following the direction of material flow @sandner1998.

The approach most similar to this thesis involves cutting through the material flow and solving different stages independently. Stage-wise decomposition for supply chains was pioneered by Clark and Scarf @clarkscarf1960, who introduced echelon-based decomposition for stochastic inventory systems, solving each stage separately through a single backward pass from downstream to upstream. Coupling between stages is handled through penalty terms in the cost function, achieving exact optimality for serial structures. This foundational work was extended in various directions @bessler1966 @glasserman1994 @chen2000 @diks1998, but these results apply to stochastic dynamic programming for pure inventory problems rather than @MILP.

Multi-echelon decomposition approaches that partition networks along functional or organizational boundaries face the challenge of coordinating the resulting subproblems @dogan1999. Barbarosoglu and Ozgur @barbarosoglu1999 decomposed production-distribution planning into a two-level hierarchy, with coupling handled through Lagrangian relaxation where multipliers serve as price signals updated via subgradient optimization. This approach requires multiple iterations and provides no direct feasibility guarantee. The work of Lidestam and Ronnqvist @lidestam2011 provides a particularly instructive case study. They decompose a supply chain into upstream procurement-production and downstream distribution subproblems, with flow variables duplicated at the boundary and Lagrangian multipliers penalizing discrepancies. Their computational experiments reveal the limitation that even after ten iterations, solutions frequently remain infeasible or exhibit gaps ranging from approximately one percent to over seventy percent, with several instances producing no feasible solution at all.

The dominant coordination mechanism in the literature is Lagrangian-based price coordination @everett1963 @lemarechal2001 @barbarosoglu1999 @lidestam2011 @jackson2003. Lagrange multipliers are iteratively updated through subgradient optimization or bundle methods. These methods share common limitations as subproblems must be solved multiple times as coordination signals converge and solutions to relaxed subproblems may violate coupling constraints that were relaxed, requiring heuristic repair procedures whose success cannot be assured @bertsekas1999.

Existing domain-level decomposition approaches thus share the limitations that they require iterative coordination between subproblems and they provide no inherent feasibility guarantee. This thesis addresses this research gap with a graph-structural decomposition approach that exploits supply chain topology to coordinate subproblems through quantity-based coupling in a single up-and-down pass. Instead of price signals requiring iterative adjustment, the approach enforces hard flow constraints to maintain feasibility and uses objective function penalties to drive towards better solutions. This eliminates the iteration requirement while guaranteeing that the composed solution satisfies all constraints across subnetwork boundaries.
